WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.159
The context window is the boundary on every Claude inference call.

2
00:00:04.159 --> 00:00:06.880
In this lesson, we'll cover what a token is,

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.199
what actually counts against the window,

4
00:00:09.199 --> 00:00:12.319
how different content types tokenize,

5
00:00:12.319 --> 00:00:15.800
and what the limits look like across Claude model tiers.

6
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.959
Understanding this constraint in detail is what

7
00:00:18.959 --> 00:00:23.239
separates systems that hold up to scale and ones that break under load.

8
00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:27.979
Everything the model can see in a single call lives inside the context window.

9
00:00:27.979 --> 00:00:29.719
That includes the system prompt,

10
00:00:29.739 --> 00:00:31.379
the conversation history,

11
00:00:31.379 --> 00:00:33.340
any documents you have injected,

12
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.500
the tool definitions, and the model's own output.

13
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.500
Anything outside it may as well not exist.

14
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.799
The unit of measure is the token,

15
00:00:41.799 --> 00:00:45.860
roughly four characters or three-quarters of an English word.

16
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.060
The window has a hard ceiling,

17
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:53.380
a maximum token count for one request that covers input and output together.

18
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.819
Cross it and the request either fails outright or gets truncated.

19
00:00:58.119 --> 00:01:02.080
Those boundaries govern every design decision in this lesson,

20
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.760
so it's worth being precise about them from the outset.

21
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.559
One of the most common mistakes I see in production systems is treating

22
00:01:09.559 --> 00:01:13.720
the context window as if it only contains the user's message.

23
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.879
In reality, every component of the request contributes tokens.

24
00:01:17.879 --> 00:01:20.680
Your system prompt might be 500 words.

25
00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.839
A multi-turn conversation with 20 exchanges adds up fast.

26
00:01:24.839 --> 00:01:26.220
If you're using RAG,

27
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:32.379
the retrieved document chunks can be thousands of tokens before the user says a single word.

28
00:01:32.379 --> 00:01:35.519
Tool schemas, injected metadata,

29
00:01:35.519 --> 00:01:38.319
and memory summaries all add up.

30
00:01:38.319 --> 00:01:45.279
Then the model's own output tokens count too at a higher price per token on most tiers.

31
00:01:45.279 --> 00:01:47.760
In well-instrumented production systems,

32
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.900
the actual user message is often a small fraction of total context usage.

33
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:58.500
Input and output tokens serve different functions and have different cost profiles.

34
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.980
Input tokens are everything you send.

35
00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.739
The system prompt, conversation history,

36
00:02:03.739 --> 00:02:07.980
documents, tool definitions, and the current user message.

37
00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.100
In RAG and agentic pipelines,

38
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.660
input is almost always the dominant share of total context usage.

39
00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.320
Output tokens are what the model generates in return.

40
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.559
Output tokens typically cost more per token than input,

41
00:02:22.559 --> 00:02:25.679
and they also count against the context window limit.

42
00:02:25.679 --> 00:02:27.919
This matters for chain of thought reasoning.

43
00:02:27.919 --> 00:02:31.240
When you enable extended thinking or scratchpad reasoning,

44
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:35.080
the model can generate a lot of output tokens that eat into

45
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.000
the available budget before it ever produces a final answer.

46
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.440
Tokenization isn't uniform,

47
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.199
and this affects your cost and your architecture.

48
00:02:44.199 --> 00:02:47.520
Plain English prose tokenizes efficiently,

49
00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.000
roughly one token per four characters or about three-quarters of a word.

50
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.440
Code tends to be similarly efficient because programming keywords and

51
00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:01.160
common identifiers are well-represented in the tokenizer vocabulary.

52
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:06.820
Structured formats like JSON and XML are less efficient because all those brackets,

53
00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:10.600
quotes, colons, and new lines each consume tokens.

54
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.000
Non-English text can be significantly less efficient.

55
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.679
Some languages require two to four times as many tokens

56
00:03:17.679 --> 00:03:20.779
as the same semantic content in English.

57
00:03:20.779 --> 00:03:24.059
When you're choosing a data format for injected content,

58
00:03:24.059 --> 00:03:27.699
tokenization efficiency is a real factor.

59
00:03:27.699 --> 00:03:30.380
Claude comes in multiple model tiers,

60
00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.259
and it's worth being precise about what separates them.

61
00:03:33.259 --> 00:03:36.899
Context window size isn't the main dividing line.

62
00:03:36.899 --> 00:03:38.619
Haiku is the exception here.

63
00:03:38.619 --> 00:03:41.919
It carries a smaller 200,000 token window,

64
00:03:41.919 --> 00:03:47.580
and is optimized for speed and cost on lighter tasks like single-turn classification,

65
00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:51.979
short document summarization, and fast response chatbots.

66
00:03:51.979 --> 00:03:53.899
Sonnet and Opus are different.

67
00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:57.619
Both share the same large one million token window.

68
00:03:57.619 --> 00:04:00.660
So the choice between them isn't about how much fits,

69
00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.139
it's about capability and cost.

70
00:04:03.139 --> 00:04:06.580
Sonnet gives you strong performance at a lower price

71
00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:10.720
for multi-document retrieval and everyday agentic pipelines.

72
00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.579
Opus is the most capable tier for the hardest reasoning and long horizon work.

73
00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:20.540
So match the tier to the task's difficulty and your budget,

74
00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.459
not to a bigger window.

75
00:04:22.459 --> 00:04:25.660
Overflow shows up in two distinct places,

76
00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:28.119
and the API tells you about them differently.

77
00:04:28.119 --> 00:04:30.940
The first case is input that's too large.

78
00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:33.700
If the tokens you send already exceed the window,

79
00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:35.380
the request never runs.

80
00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.019
You get a 400 error,

81
00:04:37.019 --> 00:04:41.779
a rejection saying the prompt is too long before a single output token is generated.

82
00:04:41.779 --> 00:04:43.880
The second case is subtler.

83
00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:46.720
Your input fits, generation starts,

84
00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.799
and then the model runs out of room partway through its own response.

85
00:04:50.799 --> 00:04:52.959
Here, the request doesn't error out,

86
00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:58.359
it stops early, and the stop reason comes back as model context window exceeded.

87
00:04:58.359 --> 00:05:00.000
That's a truncated answer,

88
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.200
not a failed call.

89
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.359
So your handling code has to check for it explicitly.

90
00:05:04.359 --> 00:05:09.720
Treat the upfront rejection and the midstream stop as two separate cases.

91
00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.359
A context budget is a deliberate allocation plan.

92
00:05:13.359 --> 00:05:17.000
You decide in advance how many tokens each component gets.

93
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.679
So much for the system prompt,

94
00:05:18.679 --> 00:05:19.959
so much for history,

95
00:05:19.959 --> 00:05:21.799
so much for retrieved chunks,

96
00:05:21.799 --> 00:05:23.720
and headroom for the output,

97
00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:28.359
and you enforce those limits in code rather than hoping the content fits.

98
00:05:28.359 --> 00:05:33.700
In practice, that means summarizing older turns instead of carrying them verbatim,

99
00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.799
retrieving only the chunks that matter,

100
00:05:35.799 --> 00:05:37.480
capping output tokens,

101
00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:41.760
and watching usage per request so growth doesn't creep up on you.

102
00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.119
Budgeting also protects quality.

103
00:05:44.119 --> 00:05:47.519
Anthropic calls the failure mode context rot,

104
00:05:47.519 --> 00:05:52.239
where attention dilutes as total tokens grow and recall degrades,

105
00:05:52.239 --> 00:05:54.720
even though the answer is sitting right there.

106
00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.600
The position-based version of that idea is lost in the middle,

107
00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:03.399
where content stranded in the center of a long context gets read least.

108
00:06:03.399 --> 00:06:07.359
The context window doesn't announce itself when you cross it.

109
00:06:07.359 --> 00:06:09.839
What you notice is a system that starts ignoring

110
00:06:09.839 --> 00:06:12.239
the instructions that you gave it in the beginning,

111
00:06:12.239 --> 00:06:17.760
or a call that fails outright in production on the input that happened to run long.

112
00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:22.480
That's why knowing what tokenizes into what is worth your time.

113
00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:26.000
You're budgeting a space you can't actually see,

114
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.760
so you have to pay attention to it.

115
00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:29.119
That's it for this one.

116
00:06:29.119 --> 00:06:31.559
I'll see you in the next lesson.

